hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1708","Narrated to us Salm bin Junadah, who narrated to us Waki', from Mas'ar, from Ubaidullah bin al-Qibtiyyah, from Jabir bin Samurah (RA), who said: \When we used to pray behind the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"," one of us would signal to his brother with his hand"," to his right and to his left. Then when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) completed his prayer"," he said: 'What is the matter with one of you doing this"," as if [his hand movements are] the tails of restless horses? Is it not sufficient for one of you"," or is it not enough for one of you"," to do it like this?' Then he placed his hand on his right thigh"," pointed with his finger"," then greeted with salam his brother on his right and his brother on his left.\nCommentary: This hadith is graded *Sahih* and is recorded in *Sahih al-Bukhari*. It clarifies the improper practice of some companions who would make distracting hand signals to one another during the formal prayer itself"," which the Prophet (ﷺ) condemned as unnecessary. He then demonstrated the correct manner of greeting fellow worshippers after the prayer: turning to the right and left to offer salam"," without excessive movement during the formal prayer. The chapter title further explains that a worshipper's intention when turning to offer salam is directed specifically to those on the corresponding side"," whether right or left."""
